The composition now has the registry's `yt-screen-warp` component ("On-Screen Display Effect") wired over the whole frame. `hyperframes lint` passes with 0 errors and 0 warnings, but I could not render frames to check it visually: no Chrome is installed here and the download failed.

What changed in `index.html`:

- **Overlay:** a full-frame `.yt-screen-fx` layer (scanlines, pixel grid, vignette, sheen) is the last child of `#main`, so it sits on top of both clips.
- **Tilt:** `#main` carries the component's `.yt-screen-warp3d` class, a slight 3D perspective tilt of the whole frame. It should leave a thin wedge of background along the left edge; remove the class from `#main` if you want scanlines only.
- **Timing:** the overlay fades in over the first 0.5s and stays on to the end, using the component's `ytScreenOn` helper at t=0. Frame 0 itself has no scanlines yet.
- **Timeline:** the file had no GSAP timeline, which lint flagged as an error before I started. I added GSAP and registered a `main` timeline, which the component needs anyway.
- **Sizing:** `#main` now has an explicit 1920×1080 size and `position: relative` so the overlay covers the frame.

The component was installed with `hyperframes add` to `compositions/components/yt-screen-warp.html`.

`hyperframes catalog --query` returned no results. The registry in `hyperframes.json` (`https://localhost:4890/registry`) lists 455 items but only serves 4 of them, so I found this one by reading the item metadata directly. I did not file a catalog gap report, since the item exists.

