{"line":4,"escaped":"\\u{000A}","points":[[1,"U+000A"]]}
{"line":5,"escaped":"- **Overlay:** a full-frame `.yt-screen-fx` layer (scanlines, pixel grid, vignette, sheen) is the last child of `#main`, so it sits on top of both clips.\\u{000A}","points":[[154,"U+000A"]]}
{"line":6,"escaped":"- **Tilt:** `#main` carries the component's `.yt-screen-warp3d` class, a slight 3D perspective tilt of the whole frame. It should leave a thin wedge of background along the left edge; remove the class from `#main` if you want scanlines only.\\u{000A}","points":[[242,"U+000A"]]}
{"line":7,"escaped":"- **Timing:** the overlay fades in over the first 0.5s and stays on to the end, using the component's `ytScreenOn` helper at t=0. Frame 0 itself has no scanlines yet.\\u{000A}","points":[[167,"U+000A"]]}
{"line":8,"escaped":"- **Timeline:** the file had no GSAP timeline, which lint flagged as an error before I started. I added GSAP and registered a `main` timeline, which the component needs anyway.\\u{000A}","points":[[177,"U+000A"]]}
{"line":9,"escaped":"- **Sizing:** `#main` now has an explicit 1920\\u{00D7}1080 size and `position: relative` so the overlay covers the frame.\\u{000A}","points":[[47,"U+00D7"],[115,"U+000A"]]}
