--- end repository character annotation ---

The on-screen display overlay is installed and wired over the full 1920×1080 frame for the whole 10 seconds, but I couldn't render a frame to look at it. This machine has no Chrome and couldn't download one, so only `hyperframes lint` ran (0 errors, 0 warnings).

What changed:

- **Component installed:** `npx hyperframes add yt-screen-warp` (the registry's "On-Screen Display Effect") wrote `compositions/components/yt-screen-warp.html` and `hyperframes.lock.json`.
- **Overlay added in `index.html`:** a `<div id="screen-fx" class="yt-screen-fx">` is the last child of `#main`, carrying the component's scanlines, pixel grid, vignette and sheen. It is held at full opacity above every clip rather than faded in.
- **Root sized:** `#main` now has `position:relative`, 1920×1080 and `overflow:hidden` so the overlay covers exactly the frame.
- **`data-no-timeline` on the root:** I added this beyond what you asked. The composition already failed lint for having no GSAP timeline, and this attribute is the linter's own fix for a composition with no animations. Remove it if you add a timeline later.

Things to know before you rely on it:

- **Scanlines may be faint here.** The stock scanlines are 10% black lines, and your composition is white text on a `#111` background, so they should show mainly over the text and any bright footage. The sheen and vignette should carry most of the look on the dark areas. If it's too subtle in preview, raise the scanline alpha in `.yt-screen-fx`.
- **I left out the component's 3D tilt.** Tilting the whole frame would pull its edges inward and leave corners without scanlines. It suits footage inset in a wrapper; say if you want it anyway.

