The composition now has the registry's "On-Screen Display Effect" (`yt-screen-warp`) over the full 1920×1080 frame: scanlines, pixel grid, vignette and sheen, plus a slight 3D tilt. I couldn't render or preview it here (no Chrome or FFmpeg in this environment), so the look itself is unverified; `hyperframes lint` passes with 0 errors and 0 warnings.

What changed in `index.html`:

- **Overlay:** both clips now sit inside a new `#screen` wrapper, with the scanline overlay as its last child so it draws on top of everything.
- **Timing:** the overlay fades in over the first 0.5s and stays on to the end of the 10s. That is the component's own "on" cue, so frame 0 itself has no scanlines yet.
- **Tilt:** the wrapper carries the component's perspective tilt, so the whole frame is slightly angled and a thin wedge of background shows along the left edge. Removing `class="yt-screen-warp3d"` from `#screen` gives a flat, full-bleed version.
- **GSAP:** the overlay is driven by a GSAP timeline, so the page now loads GSAP from the jsdelivr CDN and registers a `main` timeline. This also clears the "missing timeline registration" lint error the project had before.

I installed the component with `hyperframes add yt-screen-warp`, which added `compositions/components/yt-screen-warp.html` and `hyperframes.lock.json` and recorded the item in `hyperframes.json`.

Decision for you: if you want the scanlines present from the very first frame rather than fading in, it's a one-line change (start the overlay at full opacity instead of cueing it). I kept the component's documented wiring; say if you want it switched.

