The grain overlay is wired into `index.html`, but I couldn't confirm it visually: there's no Chrome on this machine and the CLI's browser download was blocked, so no frame has been rendered.

- **Install:** I installed the registry's `grain-overlay` component from your project's configured registry (`https://localhost:4890/registry`) to `compositions/components/grain-overlay.html`.
- **Wiring:** the overlay `<div id="grain-overlay">` is now the last child of `#main`, covering the full frame for the whole video, and its CSS is in the existing `<style>` block.
- **Deviations from the stock snippet:** I moved its inline styles into the stylesheet and added `overflow:hidden`, so the oversized noise texture can't spill past the frame.
- **Tuning:** grain strength is `opacity:.15` and flicker speed is `.5s`, both on `#grain-overlay .grain-texture`.

`hyperframes lint` reports one error and one warning, both there before my change and left alone: the composition has no `window.__timelines` registration and no `data-no-timeline` on `#main`. The snapshot run flagged the same missing registration as an invalid composition, and lint warns it adds 45 seconds to every render. Fix that before rendering — adding `data-no-timeline` to `#main` is the lint-suggested fix if you don't plan a GSAP timeline.

To check the grain yourself, point the CLI at a Chrome install and snapshot a few frames:

```bash
export HYPERFRAMES_BROWSER_PATH=/path/to/chrome
npx hyperframes snapshot --at 1,1.1,5
```

