The video now has a film grain overlay, but I couldn't check it visually: there is no Chrome on this machine and the download was blocked, so no frames were captured.

I installed the registry's `grain-overlay` component (`hyperframes add grain-overlay`) and wired it into `index.html`:

- **Markup:** the `#grain-overlay` element is the last child of the `main` composition, so it sits above the title and caption for the full 10 seconds.
- **Styles:** the component's CSS is merged into the existing `<style>` block. The grain is at the component's defaults: 15% opacity, with the noise position jittering on a 0.5s loop.
- **One deviation from the component:** I added `overflow: hidden` to the overlay so the oversized noise texture can't spill outside the frame.

`hyperframes lint` reports one error and one warning, both from the composition having no `window.__timelines` registration. The original file had no script at all, so these aren't caused by the grain, and I left them alone. The warning says the missing registration adds a 45-second wait to every render unless `data-no-timeline` is set on the root element.

To adjust the look, change `opacity:0.15` or the `0.5s` duration on `.grain-texture` in `index.html`. The installed source is kept at `compositions/components/grain-overlay.html` for reference.

