The film grain overlay is now in `index.html`, but I couldn't see it rendered: there is no Chrome on this machine and the download was blocked, so the snapshot failed.

I installed the `grain-overlay` component from your project's registry with `hyperframes add`, then pasted it into the composition:

- **Markup:** a full-frame `#grain-overlay` div is the last child of `#main`, sitting above the title and caption at `z-index: 100`.
- **Styles:** the grain texture and its `hf-grain-noise` animation are in a second `<style>` block in the head. The grain is at 0.15 opacity and shifts on a 0.5s loop.
- **One deviation from the registry snippet:** I added `overflow: hidden` to the overlay so the oversized texture (200% of the frame) can't create scrollable overflow.

The install also created `compositions/components/grain-overlay.html`, `hyperframes.lock.json` and a `.hyperframes/` cache folder, and added a `registryItems` entry to `hyperframes.json`.

`hyperframes lint` reports one error and one warning, both of which were there before my change: the composition has no `window.__timelines` registration and no `data-no-timeline` attribute. I left those alone. The linter says the missing attribute adds a 45-second wait to every render, so it's worth adding `data-no-timeline` to `#main` if you don't plan a GSAP timeline.

To check the grain yourself, run `npx hyperframes preview`, or point `HYPERFRAMES_BROWSER_PATH` at a Chrome install and run `npx hyperframes snapshot`.

