The video now has a film grain overlay, but I couldn't render a frame to check it visually: there is no Chrome in this environment and the download was blocked.

I installed the `grain-overlay` component from your project's registry with `npx hyperframes add grain-overlay`, then wired it into `index.html`:

- **Overlay element:** a full-frame `#grain-overlay` div is the last child of `#main`, layered above the title and caption for the whole 10 seconds.
- **Styles:** the grain keyframes and texture CSS sit in a new `<style>` block in the head.
- **One deviation from the registry snippet:** I added `overflow:hidden` to the overlay so the oversized, jittering texture can't spill outside the frame or cause scrollbars in preview.

`npx hyperframes lint` reports one error and one warning, both about the composition having no `window.__timelines` registration (the 45-second render delay the warning describes can be skipped with `data-no-timeline` on the root). Neither comes from the grain markup, and I left them alone.

The grain is at the component default of 0.15 opacity and a 0.5s jitter cycle; both are in the `#grain-overlay .grain-texture` rule if you want it subtler or heavier.

The install also created `compositions/components/grain-overlay.html` (the reference copy), `hyperframes.lock.json`, a `registryItems` entry in `hyperframes.json`, and a `.hyperframes/` local state folder with telemetry enabled by default.

