answer:
The data chart block is installed and placed on the timeline from 2s to 8s, but I couldn't render a frame to check it visually: there's no Chrome on this machine and the download was blocked.

I installed the block with `hyperframes add data-chart` (the `NAME` argument, from your configured registry), which wrote `compositions/data-chart.html`. I then added this clip to `index.html`:

```html
<div id="data-chart" class="clip" data-composition-id="data-chart" data-composition-src="compositions/data-chart.html" data-start="2" data-duration="6" data-track-index="3" data-width="1920" data-height="1080"></div>
```

`hyperframes timeline` shows it at 2–8s on its own lane, and `hyperframes lint` reports nothing new. Lint does still fail on one error that was there before I started: `main` has no `window.__timelines` registration.

Things to know before you render:

- **The animation is cut short.** The block is built for 15 seconds and its reveal runs about 9. At 6 seconds the bars finish, but the conversion line is only about half drawn and the source line never appears. Fixing this means either a longer clip or compressing the block's timings; say if you want either.
- **It overlaps your other clips.** The chart is a full-frame block with an opaque off-white background, and it shares screen time with "Launch" (2–3s) and "Welcome" (3–7s). I haven't seen how they stack.
- **Fonts may fall back.** The block loads Libre Baskerville and Libre Franklin from Google Fonts, and the compiler warned it has no local mapping for them. Without network access at render time, the text will use fallback fonts.
- **TLS check was disabled for the install.** Your registry is `https://localhost:4890`, and I ran the install with certificate verification turned off for that one command.

The install also updated `hyperframes.json` (a `registryItems` entry) and created `hyperframes.lock.json`, plus tool state under `.hyperframes/` and `.cache/`.

